"Internal Error ()\nciteturn194view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1051200417301380\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1051200417301380: (403) Forbidden\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn194view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/1308.6463v2\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://arxiv.org/html/1308.6463v2: (404) Not Found\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://arxiv.org/pdf/0711.0330)\nciteturn194view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 7; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0711.0330\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1009\nL0@P0: arXiv:0711.0330v1 [astro-ph] 2 Nov 2007\nL1@P0: Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. 000, 1–7 (2007) Printed 13 February 2013 (MN LATEX style file v2.2)\nL2@P0: Assessing statistical significance of periodogram peaks\nL3@P0: R.V. Baluev\nL4@P0: 1⋆\nL5@P0: 1Sobolev Astronomical Institute, Saint Petersburg State University,\nL6@P0: Universitetskij prospekt 28, Petrodvorets, Saint Petersburg 198504, Russia\nL7@P0: Accepted 2007 November –. Received 2007 October 16; in original form 2007 June 13\nL8@P0: ABSTRACT\nL9@P0: The least-squares (or Lomb-Scargle) periodogram is a powerful tool which is used rou\u0002tinely in many branches of astronomy to search for periodicities in observational data.\nL10@P0: The problem of assessing statistical significance of candidate periodicities for different\nL11@P0: periodograms is considered. Based on results in extreme value theory, improved an\u0002alytic estimations of false alarm probabilities are given. They include an upper limit\nL12@P0: to the false alarm probability (or a lower limit to the significance). These estimations\nL13@P0: are tested numerically in order to establish regions of their practical applicability.\nL14@P0: Key words: methods: data analysis - methods: statistical - surveys\nL15@P0: 1 INTRODUCTION\nL16@P0: Analysing astronomical time series, one often has to choose\nL17@P0: between at least two hypotheses, a base oneH and an alter\u0002native one K, based on the existing data array. In the signal\nL18@P0: detection problem, one should check whether the observa\u0002tions are consistent with some base model or they contain an\nL19@P0: extra deterministic signal. Under presence of random errors,\nL20@P0: such problem can be solved in a probabilistic sense only. We\nL21@P0: are never protected from mistakes of two kinds. They are the\nL22@P0: false retraction ofH (the ‘false alarm’) and the false non\u0002retraction of H (the false non-detection). False alarms are\nL23@P0: generally believed to be more dangerous, hence the problem\nL24@P0: of estimation of the false alarm probability (hereafter FAP)\nL25@P0: associated with a candidate signal is very important. Given\nL26@P0: some small critical value FAP\nL27@P0: ∗ (between 10\nL28@P0: −3\nL29@P0: and 0.1 usu\u0002ally), we could claim that the candidate signal is statistically\nL30@P0: significant (if its FAP< FAP\nL31@P0: ∗) or is not (FAP\nL32@P0: > FAP\nL33@P0: ∗).\nL34@P0: For the Lomb (1976) – Scargle (1982) periodogram\nL35@P0: (hereafter also L–S), the base hypothesis is that the observa\u0002tions are pure zero-mean uncorrelated and Gaussian errors\nL36@P0: (also called the white Gaussian noise). The alternative one is\nL37@P0: that a sinuous harmonic is also present. Every single value of\nL38@P0: the L–S periodogram represents a test statistic for the corre\u0002sponding problem of hypotheses testing. In routine practical\nL39@P0: cases, however, the period of a possible signal is not known a\nL40@P0: priory and we have to scan many periodogram values within\nL41@P0: a wide frequency range. In this case, the FAP is provided by\nL42@P0: the probability distribution of the maximum periodogram\nL43@P0: value under the base hypothesis (i.e., without signal in the\nL44@P0: data). Existing methods of calculating this distribution for a\nL45@P0: continuous frequency range require time-consuming Monte\u0002Carlo simulations. The aim of the present paper is to propose ⋆ E-mail: roman@astro.spbu.ru\nL46@P0: analytic approximations which could allow to avoid Monte\u0002Carlo simulations (at least in many practical cases). Such\nL47@P0: approximations of the distribution of the maximum have al\u0002ready been constructed by mathematicians specializing in\nL48@P0: the field of extreme values of random processes. In the Sec\u0002tion 3, these results are adapted for and extended to the\nL49@P0: specific features of the periodogram analysis of astronomi\u0002cal time series. In the Section 4, numerical simulations are\nL50@P0: used to explore the quality of the analytic results and to\nL51@P0: show regions of their practical applicability.\nL52@P0: 2 GENERAL FORMULATIONS\nL53@P0: Let us recover the principles of the periodogram analysis in\nL54@P0: a somewhat more general formulation than usually.\nL55@P0: 1\nL56@P0: Letx\nL57@P0: 1, x2 . . . xN be observations made at\nL58@P0: N epochs\nL59@P0: t\nL60@P0: 1, t2, . . . tN . The errors of\nL61@P0: x\nL62@P0: i are assumed to be indepen\u0002dent and Gaussian with standard deviations σi. Each value\nL63@P0: of the periodogram can be recovered as a test statistic that\nL64@P0: allows to conclude, how likely is the hypothesis that the\nL65@P0: data contain a signal of a given frequencyf. Mathemat\u0002ically we should check, whether the observations are fit\u0002ted well by some base model having only dH free param\u0002eters θH, or they require an enlarged model of dK pa\u0002rameters θK = {θH, θ} with d = dK − dH parameters θ\nL66@P0: of an extra periodic signal. We will assume that for any\nL67@P0: fixed frequency both models are linear and construct them\nL68@P0: by means ofd\nL69@P0: H and\nL70@P0: d\nL71@P0: K base functions forming vectors\nL72@P0: ϕ\nL73@P0: H\nL74@P0: (t) andϕ\nL75@P0: K\nL76@P0: (t, f) ={ϕ\nL77@P0: H\nL78@P0: (t),ϕ(t, f)}. Thus the base fit\nL79@P0: model isµ\nL80@P0: H\nL81@P0: (t,θ\nL82@P0: H) =\nL83@P0: θ\nL84@P0: H\nL85@P0: ·ϕ\nL86@P0: H\nL87@P0: (t), the model of the sig\u0002nal is µ(t, θ, f) = θ · ϕ(t, f) and the complete fit model 1 Several mathematical notations, used in the present paper, are\nL88@P0-1: described in the Appendix A.2 R.V. Baluev\nL89@P1: is µK(t, θK, f) = θK · ϕK(t, f) = µH(t, θH) + µ(t, θ, f). We\nL90@P1: wish to test, whether the hypothesis H : θ = 0 should be\nL91@P1: rejected in favour of the alternative K(f) : θ 6= 0.\nL92@P1: For the L–S periodogram dH = 0, d = 2, and the signal\nL93@P1: model is given by a harmonic function θ1 cos ωt + θ2 sin ωt\nL94@P1: (here ω = 2πf). Schwarzenberg-Czerny (1998a,b) consid\u0002ered cases with dH = 0 and arbitrary d. Ferraz-Mello (1981)\nL95@P1: put dH = 1 and added a floating constant term to the har\u0002monic model with d = 2, whereas Cumming et al. (1999)\nL96@P1: accounted also for possible linear trend (dH = 2).\nL97@P1: An optimal statistical test, solving such problem in gen\u0002eral, is developed rather well (Lehman 1979, chapter 7). At\nL98@P1: first, one should compute the minima (by θH,K) of the func\u0002tion χ\nL99@P1: 2 =\nL100@P1: ˙\nL101@P1: (x − µK)\nL102@P1: 2\nL103@P1: ¸\nL104@P1: under hypotheses H and K(f). This\nL105@P1: may be done by means of any accessible linear least-squares\nL106@P1: algorithm (see also Schwarzenberg-Czerny 1998a,b). If σi are\nL107@P1: known precisely, both minima χ\nL108@P1: 2\nL109@P1: H and χ\nL110@P1: 2\nL111@P1: K(f) can be com\u0002puted and the least-squares periodogram may be defined as\nL112@P1: an advance in χ\nL113@P1: 2 provided by the transition from H to K(f):\nL114@P1: z(f) = ˆχ\nL115@P1: 2\nL116@P1: H − χ\nL117@P1: 2\nL118@P1: K(f)\nL119@P1: ˜\nL120@P1: /2. (1)\nL121@P1: The error variances are often not known precisely and we\nL122@P1: have to estimate them from the time series, explicitly or\nL123@P1: implicitly. It is usually assumed that σi = κσmes,i, where\nL124@P1: the ‘measured’ uncertainties σmes,i determine the weighting\nL125@P1: pattern of the time series, whereas the coefficient κ is uncon\u0002strained. In this case, only the ratio χ\nL126@P1: 2\nL127@P1: H/χ2K can be computed\nL128@P1: exactly, and the periodogram (1) has to be modified. We will\nL129@P1: consider the following modified periodograms:\nL130@P1: z1(f) = NH\nL131@P1: χ\nL132@P1: 2\nL133@P1: H − χ\nL134@P1: 2\nL135@P1: K(f)\nL136@P1: 2χ\nL137@P1: 2\nL138@P1: H\nL139@P1: , z2(f) = NK\nL140@P1: χ\nL141@P1: 2\nL142@P1: H − χ\nL143@P1: 2\nL144@P1: K(f)\nL145@P1: 2χ\nL146@P1: 2\nL147@P1: K(f)\nL148@P1: ,\nL149@P1: z3(f) = NK\nL150@P1: 2\nL151@P1: ln χ\nL152@P1: 2\nL153@P1: H\nL154@P1: χ\nL155@P1: 2\nL156@P1: K(f)\nL157@P1: . (2)\nL158@P1: Here, NH = N − dH and NK = N − dK are the numbers\nL159@P1: of degrees of freedom in χ\nL160@P1: 2\nL161@P1: H and χ\nL162@P1: 2\nL163@P1: K, correspondingly. The\nL164@P1: periodograms z1(f) and z2(f) are the well-known normaliza\u0002tions of z(f) by variances of residuals under the respective\nL165@P1: hypotheses. All periodograms (2) are entirely equivalent be\u0002cause they are unique functions of each other:\nL166@P1: 2z1/NH = 1 − e\nL167@P1: −2z3/NK , 2z2/NK = e2z3/NK − 1,\nL168@P1: (1 − 2z1/NH)(1 + 2z2/NK) = 1. (3)\nL169@P1: 3 FALSE ALARM PROBABILITY\nL170@P1: Let us pick any of the periodograms introduced above, and\nL171@P1: denote it as Z(f). If the frequency of a possible signal was\nL172@P1: known, the false alarm probability could be retrieved as\nL173@P1: FAPsingle = 1 − Psingle(Z), where Psingle(Z) is the cumu\u0002lative distribution function of Z(f) (taken under the base\nL174@P1: hypothesis). Under the hypothesis H, the statistic 2z follows\nL175@P1: a χ\nL176@P1: 2\nL177@P1: -distribution with d degrees of freedom, 2z2/d obeys a\nL178@P1: Fisher-Snedecor F-distribution with d and NK degrees of\nL179@P1: freedom, and 2z1/NH obeys a beta distribution with the\nL180@P1: same numbers of degrees of freedom (Lehman 1979, §7.1).\nL181@P1: Using relations (3), the distribution function of z3 can be\nL182@P1: derived easily. The corresponding expressions of false alarm\nL183@P1: probability for d = 2 are given in Table 1. Note that the\nL184@P1: third modified periodogram obeys exactly the same distri\u0002bution as the basic one, if d = 2.\nL185@P1: Now let us assume that we scan all frequencies from the\nL186@P1: interval [0, fmax] and look for the maximum value Zmax =\nL187@P1: max[0,fmax] Z(f). Then the false alarm probability, associ\u0002ated with this maximum, is FAPmax = 1−Pmax(Zmax, fmax),\nL188@P1: where Pmax(Zmax, fmax) denotes the cumulative distribu\u0002tion function of Zmax (under the base hypothesis). Pre\u0002cise expression for the latter distribution is not known\nL189@P1: even for equally spaced time series. It is always possi\u0002ble to use Monte-Carlo simulations to obtain this func\u0002tion, but this way is very time-consuming, especially for\nL190@P1: the most important region of low false alarm probabili\u0002ties (high significances). The function Pmax(Z, fmax) is often\nL191@P1: computed (Schwarzenberg-Czerny 1998a,b) as\nL192@P1: Pmax(Z, fmax) ≈ Psingle(Z)\nL193@P1: Nind(fmax)\nL194@P1: , (4)\nL195@P1: where Nind(fmax) is an effective ‘number of independent fre\u0002quencies’ found within [0, fmax]. There is no general analytic\nL196@P1: expression for the quantity Nind, but it is often suggested to\nL197@P1: use a short Monte-Carlo simulation to assess it and then ex\u0002trapolate (4) to low FAP (Cumming 2004; Horne & Baliunas\nL198@P1: 1986). However, the multiple-trial formula (4) is only heuris\u0002tic and is not necessarily precise even for equally spaced\nL199@P1: observations which don’t produce significant aliasing.\nL200@P1: A better estimation of Pmax(Z, fmax) may be obtained\nL201@P1: using the theory of stochastic processes. The theory of ex\u0002tremes of random processes is developed in mathematical\nL202@P1: literature rather deeply. For our aims, it is worth to men\u0002tion the series of works by Davies (1977, 1987, 2002). This\nL203@P1: author considered (in rather general formulations) extreme\nL204@P1: value distributions for χ\nL205@P1: 2\nL206@P1: , F, and beta random processes\nL207@P1: that may include our periodograms z and z1,2 as special\nL208@P1: cases. The main result of these works is an analytic lower\nL209@P1: limit to the corresponding extreme value distributions. This\nL210@P1: result is potentially very useful for astronomical applica\u0002tions, because it yields directly an upper limit to the false\nL211@P1: alarm probability and a lower limit to the significance of\nL212@P1: a candidate periodicity. However, the formulae published in\nL213@P1: the cited papers are not yet ready for usage and require some\nL214@P1: adaptation to specific applications. Moreover, these results\nL215@P1: can be improved to obtain not only an upper limit, but an\nL216@P1: uniform approximation to the false alarm probability, that\nL217@P1: would be good for low spectral leakage at least.\nL218@P1: A brief description of these results, adapted for the un\u0002even time series analysis, along with detailes of my exten\u0002sions, is given in the Appendix B. Summarizing them, the\nL219@P1: ‘Davies bound’ may be written down as\nL220@P1: FAPmax(Z, fmax) 6 FAPsingle(Z) + τ (Z, fmax). (5)\nL221@P1: The function τ will be specified below. If the aliasing effects\nL222@P1: may be neglected within the frequency band being scanned2,\nL223@P1: and if also fmax is large enough, then\nL224@P1: Pmax(Z, fmax) ≈ Psingle(Z) e\nL225@P1: −τ(Z,fmax)\nL226@P1: . (6)\nL227@P1: The right hand side in (5) should approach the false alarm\nL228@P1: probability more closely for large Z (even the asymptotic\nL229@P1: equality under Z → ∞ is expected, but not proved strictly\nL230@P1: yet). In general, the quantity τ (Z, fmax) looks like\nL231@P1: 2 This means that the spectral window of the time series has no\nL232@P1: significant peaks in the doubled frequency band [0, 2fmax], except\nL233@P1-2: for the main one at f = 0.Assessing statistical significance of periodogram peaks 3\nL234@P2: τ =\nL235@P2: “\nL236@P2: z\nL237@P2: π\nL238@P2: ” d−1\nL239@P2: 2 e\nL240@P2: −z\nL241@P2: 2π\nL242@P2: A(fmax) (7)\nL243@P2: for the basic least-squares periodogram (1) and like\nL244@P2: τ =\nL245@P2: γ\nL246@P2: 2π\nL247@P2: A(fmax) ×\nL248@P2: 8\nL249@P2: >>>>><\nL250@P2: >>>>>:\nL251@P2: “\nL252@P2: 2z1\nL253@P2: πNH\nL254@P2: ” d−1\nL255@P2: 2\nL256@P2: “\nL257@P2: 1 −\nL258@P2: 2z1\nL259@P2: NH\nL260@P2: ” NK−1\nL261@P2: 2\nL262@P2: ,\nL263@P2: “\nL264@P2: 2z2\nL265@P2: πNK\nL266@P2: ” d−1\nL267@P2: 2\nL268@P2: “\nL269@P2: 1 + 2z2\nL270@P2: NK\nL271@P2: ”−\nL272@P2: NH2 +1\nL273@P2: ,\nL274@P2: “\nL275@P2: 2\nL276@P2: π\nL277@P2: sh z3\nL278@P2: NK\nL279@P2: ” d−1\nL280@P2: 2\nL281@P2: e\nL282@P2: −z3\nL283@P2: “\nL284@P2: 1+ d−3\nL285@P2: 2NK\nL286@P2: ”\nL287@P2: .\nL288@P2: (8)\nL289@P2: for the modified periodograms (2). Here the coefficient\nL290@P2: γ = Γ “\nL291@P2: NH\nL292@P2: 2\nL293@P2: ” .Γ“\nL294@P2: NK+1\nL295@P2: 2\nL296@P2: ”\nL297@P2: . Note that the asymptotic\nL298@P2: (2/NH,K)\nL299@P2: (d−1)/2\nL300@P2: γ → 1 holds true for N → ∞. The factor\nL301@P2: A(fmax) depends on the bases ϕ and ϕH, on the time series\nL302@P2: sampling and on the weighting pattern. Unfortunately, the\nL303@P2: general form of A(fmax), obtained in the Appendix B, is not\nL304@P2: simple. For now, let us restrict ourselves to the L–S peri\u0002odograms and neglect by aliasing effects. In the next section\nL305@P2: we will show that such approximation for A(fmax) works well\nL306@P2: even for strong aliasing. Of course, anyone is welcome to cal\u0002culate A(fmax) numerically from the formulae given in the\nL307@P2: Appendix: such work is still much less computationaly ex\u0002pensive than Monte-Carlo simulation of Pmax(Z, fmax). The\nL308@P2: practical quality of the expressions (5,6) will be also explored\nL309@P2: numerically in the next section.\nL310@P2: To derive A(fmax) from the formula (B7), we should\nL311@P2: calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix M, which is defined\nL312@P2: by the group of equalities (B4). To perform this, we have\nL313@P2: to concretize the functions ϕ(t, f). For the usual L–S peri\u0002odogram the harmonic base\nL314@P2: ϕ(t, f) = {cos ωt, sin ωt} (ω = 2πf) (9)\nL315@P2: produces the matrices\nL316@P2: Q =\nL317@P2: 1\nL318@P2: 2\nL319@P2: „\nL320@P2: 1 + cos 2ωt sin 2ωt\nL321@P2: sin 2ωt 1 − cos 2ωt «\nL322@P2: ,\nL323@P2: S = π\nL324@P2: „\nL325@P2: −t sin 2ωt t¯+ t cos 2ωt\nL326@P2: −t¯+ t cos 2ωt t sin 2ωt «\nL327@P2: ,\nL328@P2: R = 2π\nL329@P2: 2\nL330@P2: „\nL331@P2: t\nL332@P2: 2 − t2 cos 2ωt −t2 sin 2ωt\nL333@P2: −t\nL334@P2: 2 sin 2ωt t2 + t2 cos 2ωt «\nL335@P2: ,\nL336@P2: M = Q\nL337@P2: −1\nL338@P2: (R − S\nL339@P2: TQ−1\nL340@P2: S). (10)\nL341@P2: If we consider the alias-free case, the terms in (10) contain\u0002ing sine and cosine functions of frequencies 2f 6 2fmax are\nL342@P2: averaged out. Under this approximation M ≈ 4π\nL343@P2: 2DtI, where\nL344@P2: Dt = t\nL345@P2: 2 − t¯2\nL346@P2: is the weighted variance of the observational\nL347@P2: epochs. Then both eigenvalues required are equal to the con\u0002stant 4π\nL348@P2: 2Dt and A(fmax) ≈ 2π3/2W, where W = fmaxTeff is\nL349@P2: a rescaled frequency bandwidth and Teff =\nL350@P2: √\nL351@P2: 4πDt is an ef\u0002fective time series length. If ti are spanned uniformly and all\nL352@P2: σi are equal, then Teff almost coincides with an actual time\nL353@P2: series span. Table 1 contains the alias-free approximations\nL354@P2: of τ (Z, fmax) for all L–S periodograms considered. One may\nL355@P2: use these expressions and the on